"Weighing Binary Stars | VirtualDemos",
"Weighing Binary Stars",
"Textbook: OpenStax <em>Astronomy 2e</em>, §{#} (measuring stellar masses). Access for free at https://openstax.org/details/books/astronomy-2e. The physics behind it: OpenStax <em>University Physics</em>, Vol. {#}, §{#} (Kepler’s laws) and §{#} (center of mass), and Vol. {#}, §{#} (the Doppler effect for light). Access for free at https://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-{#} and https://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-{#}.",
"<strong>Setups in the demo:</strong> Star and planet, with its own setups; Two stars, with Sun and red dwarf; Twin stars; Hot star, cool giant; Partial eclipses; Eccentric pair; Alpha Centauri A and B; Seesaw: {#} and {#} suns; Spectroscopic pair; and a Mystery system with its own button. This worksheet uses <strong>Two stars</strong>, which the link opens.",
"Two stars orbit their center of mass. The top view (or 3D) shows the orbit from above. Beside it is what a telescope sees, picked with the buttons <strong>Eclipsing</strong>, <strong>Visual</strong> (both stars seen apart on the sky) and <strong>Spectroscopic</strong> (the stars too close to see apart: only their combined spectrum). Below are the light curve and both stars’ velocities along our line of sight.",
"<strong>Play</strong> and <strong>Pause</strong> run time. To move in time, drag either graph, or click a graph and use the left and right arrow keys; Shift with an arrow key steps a twentieth of an orbit.",
"<strong>Weigh the stars</strong> in the panel lists what the demo works out from the orbit. Work your own numbers first, then check them against it.",
"<strong>Record reading</strong> stores the system and its measurements at the cursor’s time. <strong>Export to worksheet</strong> sends the readings to the tables below; <strong>Clear</strong> starts a new series.",
"The model: two stars in a Kepler orbit about their center of mass, with Newton’s form of Kepler’s third law. Alpha Centauri’s orbit and masses are measured values (Akeson et al. {#}); the seesaw and spectroscopic pairs are made up. The spectroscopic view shows hydrogen’s red line from each star, shifted by the Doppler effect.",
"Weigh two stars that orbit each other: get their total mass from the size and period of the orbit, split it between them with the center of mass, and read the split again from the Doppler shifts of a pair too close to see apart.",
"<strong>Orbits are scales.</strong> You can’t put a star on a scale, but you can watch something orbit it. The faster and tighter the orbit, the more mass pulls it around. For two stars, Kepler’s third law with Newton’s correction gives",
"\\[M_1 + M_2 = \\frac{a^3}{P^2},\\]",
"with \\(a\\) the average separation of the two stars in AU, \\(P\\) the time for one orbit in years, and the masses in solar masses. Check it on the Earth and the Sun: \\(a = 1\\), \\(P = 1\\), so the total is {#} solar mass. This gives only the <strong>total</strong> mass.",
"<strong>The seesaw.</strong> Both stars orbit a point between them, the <strong>center of mass</strong>, like two children balanced on a seesaw. The heavier one sits closer to the middle: if star {#} is a distance \\(a_1\\) from the center of mass and star {#} is \\(a_2\\) from it,",
"\\[M_1 a_1 = M_2 a_2,\\qquad \\frac{M_2}{M_1} = \\frac{a_1}{a_2},\\qquad a_1 + a_2 = a.\\]",
"The two stars always sit on opposite sides of the center of mass and take the same time to go around, so the star on the bigger circle also moves faster. Their velocities are always opposite, in the same ratio: \\(v_2 = -(M_1/M_2)\\,v_1\\).",
"<strong>Three kinds of binaries.</strong> In a <strong>visual binary</strong> a telescope shows both stars, and over years we watch them go around; that gives \\(a\\) and \\(P\\). In a <strong>spectroscopic binary</strong> the stars are too close to see apart, but each star’s spectral lines shift back and forth as it orbits, toward red as it moves away from us and toward blue as it comes toward us. A line of rest wavelength \\(\\lambda_0\\) seen at \\(\\lambda\\) gives the star’s speed along our line of sight:",
"\\[v = c\\,\\frac{\\lambda - \\lambda_0}{\\lambda_0},\\qquad c = 3.00\\times10^5\\ \\text{km/s}.\\]",
"The demo uses hydrogen’s red line, \\(\\lambda_0 = 656.28\\) nm. In an <strong>eclipsing binary</strong> the stars pass in front of each other and the light dips, which gives their sizes.",
"On the main sequence, a star’s luminosity follows its mass roughly as \\(L \\approx M^4\\) (solar units).",
"Alpha Centauri A and B, the nearest stars like the Sun, orbit each other with an average separation of about {#} AU and a period of about {#} years. What is their total mass?",
